WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
He poured cooking oil into the sea, with

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.680
a pirate ship so close behind them he

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.160
could hear the pirates laughing. His

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.880
captain grabs him by the collar and

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:10.560
goes, "We're about to get boarded, and

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.200
you're making soup?" But he doesn't even

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.200
look up. He just points at the black

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.120
clouds. "Steer into the storm." The

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.160
captain yells that they'll never survive

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:20.320
it, and all he gets back is, "Trust the

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.920
oil." But then a grappling hook clangs

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.560
off the railing. So the captain turns

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.440
the wheel and the pirates couldn't be

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:27.520
happier. Why waste a cannonball when the

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.400
storm will do it for free? Deeper in,

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.320
the waves get taller than the mast, and

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.520
the crew is tying themselves to anything

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.480
bolted down, but he's still pouring, a

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:37.440
little at a time, always on the side the

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:39.720
wind is coming from. Then the pirates

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.000
swing in to board, and up ahead a wave

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:44.120
big enough to swallow both ships rises

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.040
out of the dark. You see, sailors really

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:48.160
carried oil just for this. They called

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:50.280
it storm oil. Ever blown on a bowl of

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:52.240
soup? The broth ripples, but the oily

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.600
spots stay perfectly smooth, because

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.440
your breath can't grab [music] them, and

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.120
a wave can't crash if the wind can't

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.000
grab it. So when the wave reaches their

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.000
oily patch of sea, it just lifts the

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.320
ship and sets it down. And 50 yards

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.120
behind, it curls over and drops straight

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:06.120
onto the pirates'

